      "order": 0,
      "mode": 0,
      "title": "Note",
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "# Status: verified working on this box (2026-08-24)\n\nAll four model slots are present and were checked **by tensor layout**, not by\nfilename:\n\n| slot | file | verified |\n|---|---|---|\n| diffusion model | `wan2.1_t2v_14B_fp8_e4m3fn.safetensors` / `wan2.1_i2v_480p_14B_fp8_e4m3fn.safetensors` | \u2705 |\n| text encoder | `umt5_xxl_fp8_e4m3fn_scaled.safetensors` | \u2705 loads as `WanTEModel`, 6419 MB |\n| VAE | `wan_2.1_vae.safetensors` | \u2705 |\n| CLIP-Vision (I2V) | `clip_vision_h.safetensors` | \u2705 1208 MB |\n| speed LoRA | `Wan21_I2V_14B_lightx2v_cfg_step_distill_lora_rank64.safetensors` | \u2705 native `diffusion_model.*` keys |\n\n## \u26a0 `weight_dtype` must stay on `fp8_e4m3fn` \u2014 this is not cosmetic\n\nThe checkpoint is fp8 on disk (15.3 GB). With `weight_dtype = default`, ComfyUI\n**upcasts it to fp16 on load**: the log reads\n\n```\nRequested to load WAN21\nloaded completely; 31269.80 MB loaded     <-- 30.5 GB, not 15.3\n```\n\nAdd the 6.3 GB encoder, 1.2 GB CLIP-Vision and 0.5 GB VAE and you are at ~38.5 GB\nof weights before a single activation is allocated. On 2026-08-24 that OOM-killed\nComfyUI outright:\n\n```\nkernel: Out of memory: Killed process (python) anon-rss:31872944kB\n```\n\nand on the retry it survived only by swapping \u2014 step 1 ran at **4.6 s/it**, step 2\nat **149 s/it**, step 3 at **197 s/it**. A 40\u00d7 cliff like that is always thrash,\nnever compute.\n\n`fp8_e4m3fn` keeps the transformer at its native ~15 GB and halves the footprint.\n\n## The other memory lever: the text encoder\n\n`CLIPLoader device = cpu` keeps umT5 (6.3 GB) off the GPU allocator. It encodes\nonce per prompt, so the CPU round-trip costs seconds and buys back 6 GB for the\npart that runs 81 \u00d7 steps times.\n\nIf you are still tight, the order to cut is: **frames \u2192 resolution \u2192 steps.**\nFrames are the cheapest thing to give up and the most expensive thing to keep.\n\n# Wan 2.1 \u2014 image to video\n\n## The matched set \u2014 change one, change all five\n\n| slot | value |\n|---|---|\n| text encoder | `umt5_xxl_fp8_e4m3fn_scaled.safetensors` \u2014 `CLIPLoader type = **wan**`, `device = cpu` |\n| VAE | `wan_2.1_vae.safetensors` (16-ch; this is correct for **all** Wan 2.1 and Wan 2.2 **14B**) |\n| patch node | `ModelSamplingSD3` \u2014 **mandatory**, this is Wan's shift node |\n| latent | 16-channel video latent |\n\n`device = cpu` on the encoder keeps umT5 (\u224811 GB) off the GPU. On unified memory\nthat is a sensible trade: the text encode runs once, the transformer runs 6\u00d781.\n\n## Speed vs quality \u2014 the two paths, and they move together\n\nThe lightx2v LoRA is a **CFG-step-distilled** LoRA. Switching paths means\nchanging **three** things, not one. This is the setting people get wrong.\n\n| | LoRA | steps | CFG | shift |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **fast (shipped default)** | ON @ 0.8 | 6 | **1.0** | **5.0** |\n| quality | BYPASS the LoRA | 30 | **6.0** | **8.0** |\n\nThe negative prompt is inert at CFG 1.0, which is why it runs through\n`ConditioningZeroOut`. Taking the quality path? **Bypass the ConditioningZeroOut\ntoo** \u2014 one right-click \u2014 and the real negative comes back to life.\n\nBe realistic about the quality path on this hardware: 30 steps of a 14B\ntransformer over 81 frames is a very long run. Start on the fast path.\n\n## The 4n+1 frame rule\n\nEvery Wan latent node computes `((length - 1) // 4) + 1`. Only **1, 5, 9 \u2026 81,\n121** land on a chunk boundary; off-grid values are silently truncated and give\nyou a mangled final chunk. The widget steps by 4 to help you, but it will still\nlet you type 50.\n\n`81` frames @ 16 fps = **5.06 s**, which is the 14B\ntraining length. Going much past 81 drifts.\n\n## The CLIP-Vision asymmetry \u2014 the classic Wan trap\n\n**Wan 2.1 I2V needs `clip_vision_h`.** `CLIPVisionEncode` runs with\n**`crop = none`** (not `center` \u2014 cropping throws away the framing the model is\nbeing asked to animate) and lands on `WanImageToVideo.clip_vision_output`.\n\nWan **2.2** I2V A14B has no CLIP-Vision node at all; the socket exists and is\nleft null. So a Wan 2.2 workflow copied over here is missing a required piece,\nand a Wan 2.1 workflow copied to 2.2 has a spare one. Both fail confusingly.\n\n## Where the conditioning actually comes from\n\n`WanImageToVideo` does not just make a latent \u2014 it **rewrites the conditioning**.\nIts `positive`/`negative` outputs are what must reach the `KSampler`, not the raw\n`CLIPTextEncode` outputs. Wiring the text encoders straight to the sampler is the\nsingle most common way to get an I2V graph that runs and ignores your image.\n\n## Resolution and the source image\n\nSource is `i2v_source.jpg` \u2014 768x960, a 4:5 portrait. It is resized to **512 x 640**:\nsame 4:5 aspect, both dimensions /16-clean, and just under the 480p pixel budget\nthe 14B was trained on. `crop = center` on the resize is a no-op at matching\naspect; it only bites if you swap in an image of a different shape.\n\n## Prompting for motion, not for a picture\n\nThe still frame is already given to the model \u2014 describing it again wastes the\nprompt. Describe **what moves**: the subject's action, and the camera's.\n\n- \u2705 `He shifts his weight and turns his head slowly toward the camera; a light\n  breeze moves his hair; the camera drifts slightly left, handheld.`\n- \u274c `black and white portrait of a young man against a brick wall, 8k` \u2014 that is\n  a caption, and it buys you 5 seconds of a nearly-frozen frame.\n\nAsk for less motion than feels right. Wan 2.1 at 6 distilled steps degrades fast\nwhen asked for large, fast movement.\n"
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    },
